  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Freckled Dapperling (Echinoderma asperum) is a small woodland mushroom characterized by its delicate appearance and distinctive speckled cap surface. This member of the Verrucosporaceae family is a common sight on forest floors throughout New England during the mushroom fruiting season.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Speckled Cap Surface",
      "description": "The cap displays prominent wart-like projections or scales that give it a distinctly freckled or speckled appearance, ranging from whitish to pale brown.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Delicate Stem",
      "description": "The stem is thin, fragile, and typically features a ring or partial ring zone, with a bulbous or cup-like base (volva) at the ground.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "White Gills",
      "description": "The mushroom has crowded white gills that are free from the stem, distinguishing it from similar species.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Echinoderma asperum grows in mixed deciduous and coniferous forests throughout New England, typically appearing on rich, moist soil among leaf litter and organic debris.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'Dapperling' refers to the mushroom's neat, refined appearance, while the 'freckled' descriptor perfectly captures the distinctive spotted pattern that covers its elegant cap.",
